<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika&quot; (&quot;Lord Bless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>&quot;) was composed in 1897 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enoch-sontonga">Enoch Sontonga</a>, a Xhosa teacher at a Methodist mission school near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>. He wrote it as a hymn of a single stanza in Xhosa, in B-flat major, with four-part harmony carrying a repetitive melody that draws on both Western hymn writing and South African melody.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the 1994 general election &quot;Die Stem&quot; kept its official status, sharing it equally with &quot;<b>Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</b>&quot;, long a hymn of the ANC.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</b>&quot; (&quot;Lord Bless Africa&quot;) was composed in 1897 by Enoch Sontonga, a Xhosa teacher at a Methodist mission school near Johannesburg.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Ishe Komborera Africa&quot;, a Shona translation of Enoch Sontonga's Xhosa hymn &quot;<b>Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</b>&quot;, became Zimbabwe's first anthem, replaced in 1994 by &quot;Simudzai Mureza wedu WeZimbabwe&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rise-o-voices-of-rhodesia">Rise, O Voices of Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
